            <ArticleTitle>Eight weeks of conjugated linoleic acid supplementation has no effect on antioxidant status in healthy overweight/obese Korean individuals.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00394-011-0199-y</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to evaluate the effect of CLA supplementation on antioxidant metabolism in healthy overweight/obese Korean individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, where 29 healthy overweight/obese (BMI ≥ 23 kg/m(2)) participants (2 men and 27 women) were randomly selected to receive placebo (n = 15, 2.4 g olive oil/day) or 2.4 g/day CLA mixture (n = 14, 36.9% of cis-9, trans-11 and 37.9% of trans-10, cis-12) for 8 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were no significant differences in plasma total radical-trapping antioxidant potential (TRAP), lipid peroxidation (conjugated dienes), lipid-soluble antioxidant vitamin concentration, erythrocyte antioxidant enzyme (superoxide dismutase, catalase, glutathione peroxidase) activities, and leukocyte DNA damage between the CLA and placebo groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The data suggest that short-term supplementation (8 weeks) with CLA (2.4 g/day) might have no significant effects on lipid peroxidation and antioxidant metabolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
